From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: native macro RAM 0x3000 -> 0x8000 words so entries past 0x1000 run (Zortos, zortos293/Lichii #439, from Djihads80/Eglantine#663), EDEN_UP_MACRO_RAM_WIDE=1 (boot), off by default

Switch: EDEN_UP_MACRO_RAM_WIDE=1 (exact 1, read once when the first macro engine is made, so it
is a boot switch). Unset: 0x3000 words and the method < 0x1000 clamp, as before.

What: the Turing native macro RAM (MacroEngine::native_code) holds 0x3000 words, three per
instruction, so 0x1000 instructions. Upstream's commit message and the core gap audit: Ocarina
calls native macros that start at instructions 0x1010 and 0x1018; with the old size their code is
cut off at upload (ClearCode sends the write offset past the end for an entry >= 0x1000) and the
calls fail with "Native MME failed ... instruction outside uploaded RAM", dropping draws. The
upstream author saw constant flicker in the village and thousands of these errors a minute
before, none after. Our run149 (6 Nov) logged 31 such lines at 429 s; the 40 newest run folders
(12 Nov) had none (from the core gap audit; not checked again here). With the switch on the RAM
holds 0x8000 words (0x2aaa instructions) and ClearCode accepts every entry the RAM holds.

Where: video_core/macro.h (NativeMacroRamWords; native_code is now a std::vector sized at boot
from the switch instead of std::array<u32, 0x3000>; ClearCode's clamp is upstream's
"method * 3 < size", which is method < 0x1000 at 0x3000 words), macro.cpp and
engines/kepler_compute.cpp (the macro dumps write native_code.size() words instead of sizeof).
Everything that reads the RAM already takes a span or size() (the interpreter, the native JIT,
AnalyzeEffects, the headless MacroNative hook, the split back's copy).

Differences from upstream: upstream changes the array to std::array<u32, 0x8000> for everyone
(+80 KiB per macro engine) and adds a unit test; here the size is chosen at boot, so off costs no
memory, and the test is not taken (our tree does not build src/tests for the console).

Origin and credit: Zortos (zortos293), zortos293/Lichii commit 7969a392c2 "macro: enlarge the
Turing macro RAM so entries past 0x1000 execute (#439)", 7 Nov 2026, GPL-3.0; from Brandon
Bunnelle's Djihads80/Eglantine#663. Port and switch ours.

Off: native_code is a heap vector of the same 0x3000 words (one pointer load more per macro
upload word and per RAM read set-up); same clamp, same dumps byte for byte. No shader translation
change, no shader-store stamp change.

Cost when on: +80 KiB per macro engine (a few engines: 3D, compute and the split back's copy), dumps of 128 KiB instead
of 48 KiB, and the native JIT (when it runs) prepares 0x2aaa instructions instead of 0x1000 each
time the uploaded code changes, so a JIT prepare costs up to 2.7 times as long.

Test: count "instruction outside uploaded RAM" lines in every batch run (on: should be 0). One run
from a save in the later area where run149 saw them, if such a save exists. Look at: those lines,
flicker in the village clips, walk fps within 0.3 of off, no new crash kind.
